{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":29259,"duration_api_ms":30419,"num_turns":8,"result":"I wrote the script to `src/49-cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`. I haven't run it or checked that it parses, because the dry-run command needed approval.\n\nIt attaches to the `power:cpu_frequency` tracepoint and prints one line per frequency change, showing the time, CPU, old frequency and new frequency in MHz. When you press Ctrl-C, bpftrace prints these maps automatically:\n- `@changes`: the number of frequency changes per CPU.\n- `@time_ms`: milliseconds spent at each frequency, per CPU. The last interval before Ctrl-C isn't counted.\n- `@freq_hist`: a histogram of the target frequencies, in 200 MHz buckets.\n\nTo run it:\n```bash\nsudo bpftrace src/49-cpufreq/cpufreq.bt\n```\n\nSome cpufreq drivers don't emit this tracepoint. `intel_pstate` in active mode is one example. If you see no output, `sudo bpftrace -e 'profile:hz:10 { @[cpu] = ...}'` won't help directly. A fallback is to sample `/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq` instead.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"8dd6e06b-7060-4a38-8b47-5a6b4de0e184","total_cost_usd":0.1044425,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3402,"cache_read_input_tokens":155990,"output_tokens":2960,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3402},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":407,"cache_read_input_tokens":27479,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":351,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.000446,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{